#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157051 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/id #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21774" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157052 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" :question/math-academy-id "e-21775" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](images/db/db5806ea651ef0c5a635e5b019ad77c1dc4f93c3f8d7d0a9ae525669ff25c776.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](images/0c/0c0a62974ee1a4d6b4f35b49f36d737cdb19a2190983bb28b55a74e01daae4d6.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](images/55/55ecfbb20bb6e0166c9d676d9290c15c795ec855e2f171bc21b9df93c4bc497f.svg)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157053 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" :question/math-academy-id "e-21777" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](images/51/51f35485ec1b324b13c8d0a6e80b3c09f655f985a56ba9c5583cedb9fd4d2168.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](images/18/1885f67835b63059d8212b9d96f4a4b916c01205d0be9043722e1e16aeb06e64.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](images/c3/c3d19c5c31140ed5bb678da9defeab18bbaf712453735d147b3d5063f9137363.svg)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d746599e-d56a-5166-8351-9899cbd6e214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155403} {:answer/id #uuid "f9edff07-3deb-5d58-b03f-400e4a3df99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155404} {:answer/id #uuid "ff234df4-49fb-511a-935a-0e010d032437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155405} {:answer/id #uuid "0f59cca3-adfe-59bb-b8c2-23f07c13b46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155406} {:answer/id #uuid "6902f5e7-f3f2-5ed9-99e9-0f0a9db25252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155407}] :answer-field/id #uuid "75d6466c-ea8d-5e1a-b021-bdcde3190634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155402}] :question/id #uuid "b8407af1-3dc4-57d5-975f-7d690924fc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337171" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = xe^{2x}$.\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02aa2931-7319-5eae-9d08-6773a3ce5feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477741} {:answer/id #uuid "2766ea17-aeed-504c-a94b-bf82fe983ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477812} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d299c-6f4f-5c00-ad91-3c97e7012470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477952} {:answer/id #uuid "7db9b1cc-8c00-5908-9a9e-8f7f4b027041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477992} {:answer/id #uuid "84c0b521-9ff1-595e-bda5-2e7954af39ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2766ea17-aeed-504c-a94b-bf82fe983ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477812} :answer-field/id #uuid "489e0198-dac9-5cfe-81b0-6c9678cf7acf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "605f7436-db66-5e71-900c-b1554cd28ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-337239" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=(2{x}^{2}-1){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "331a74e8-7628-5ce4-920c-ba2960b82e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477830} {:answer/id #uuid "488074fc-db8c-5334-8161-7d87fba0eeb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477878} {:answer/id #uuid "5277712e-2c09-59e0-a129-c51bdc7931c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477905} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc0835d-5a7a-5576-8bbb-0af3fada14f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477958} {:answer/id #uuid "77dc50ab-7aa8-560f-833f-855daa986102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "331a74e8-7628-5ce4-920c-ba2960b82e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477830} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e2d776b-916a-506e-bc6f-9af1b018f8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc1320a7-5bac-56be-bf06-3e780f4ac1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337251" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y={x}^{2}-3x+1.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04642a14-4932-5f9d-a9a5-714cb02ad7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308777} {:answer/id #uuid "086ff242-9b70-5309-b9be-e93742bbce32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308812} {:answer/id #uuid "78142e29-1354-59d2-9b10-d