t-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y={e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+6(u{e}^{-6x}) & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-6u{e}^{-6x}+6u{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bd1c52-2c1a-5a9b-8db0-abe912c62f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309023} {:answer/id #uuid "37490114-9013-5a9d-b4c0-cbc2cbece88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309172} {:answer/id #uuid "5f840941-afe0-58f5-a3e5-aef3862148ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309466} {:answer/id #uuid "80b72e25-6779-58a5-ab0a-51a251ed7cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309761} {:answer/id #uuid "d61fcdb7-bd6a-5cca-984a-86000cecb81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b72e25-6779-58a5-ab0a-51a251ed7cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309761} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c647f4c-653b-51fb-b540-e1e426e7e94f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fea8e53-8238-50aa-a899-3a848803520a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309111} {:answer/id #uuid "52e2457e-8866-59a4-8053-182b261ddefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309375} {:answer/id #uuid "c630fc48-a87e-5c0e-b6df-ecfc416dafdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310341} {:answer/id #uuid "c97e80ea-2a72-5de9-9dac-f280380d1d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310363} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4b82bb-6800-5c37-a7b3-3429121a21e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1d95c6-a996-5d7f-8b91-cc36584f20d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd4b82bb-6800-5c37-a7b3-3429121a21e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} :answer-field/id #uuid "5581fbfd-c57f-55cf-aa87-2be0cc272876" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b41119c-3123-5c65-8e46-5b1b37d995e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309431} {:answer/id #uuid "92a22264-ebe8-5157-b2d1-75c2ce07b198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309919} {:answer/id #uuid "9397505f-6d1e-5019-8b08-41a8d0e962d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309930} {:answer/id #uuid "fac2e241-ae02-5170-826e-3ac765a9a7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b41119c-3123-5c65-8e46-5b1b37d995e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309431} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcad9de3-0595-5b13-b359-6cabf54fe1a0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14e95436-1992-5d26-a51a-862b1ae1c205" :question/math-academy-id "q-337533" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y={e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{6x})}^{′}-6(u{e}^{6x}) & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+6u{e}^{6x}-6u{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
